---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/intermediate/stocks-and-bonds">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Stocks and bonds are the two primitive financial claims: <strong>ownership and
    debt</strong>. Everything in markets — funds, derivatives, entire economies of speculation —
    is built from these two. Understanding what you actually hold (a share of profits, or a loan
    contract) explains both the returns and the risks.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the two claims, their prices, and the index that holds them" widget="equationStep" config={sbCfg} />

  <h2>The trade-offs, quantified</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Historical averages (US, long-run):</strong> stocks ~10%/yr nominal with brutal dips (−50%+ happens); bonds ~5%; cash ~3%. The premium is compensation for enduring the volatility.</li>
    <li><strong>Yield vs price:</strong> bond yields rise when prices fall — new buyers get more, old holders lose. The 2022 bond market (worst year in centuries) taught a generation of "safe" investors this lesson.</li>
    <li><strong>Dividends aren't free money:</strong> the share price drops by the dividend on the ex-date. Total return is what counts; "dividend investing" is a preference, not an arbitrage.</li>
    <li><strong>Time horizon decides allocation:</strong> money needed in 2 years doesn't belong in stocks (sequence risk); money needed in 30 years rarely belongs in cash (inflation risk). The money-inflation lesson sets the floor.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ibbotson SBBI historical data; Bogle, Common Sense on Mutual Funds; Malkiel, A Random Walk Down Wall Street.</p>
</TopicPage>
